Distinguish force, stress, strain, and the history producing a structure (@geo-stress-strain: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @geology, #source "../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW", #source "../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX", #source "../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N", #geology "Orientations-strike-dip-trend-and-plunge.lingua#r_4SACPSFSMY1MM5BV2QM5MJN8QR", #mining-and-mineral-engineering "../../../../stem/engineering/mining-and-mineral-engineering/ground/Rock-mass-models-intact-material-and-excavation-scale.lingua#r_1DMB4100MSM5KM3XE6FT4MADKF") { r_4TM454JHNMW1X3RF37NBDRXTXT
**Read:** [Geological Structures: a Practical Introduction — Waldron and Snyder](../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW), the complete Kinematics and Strain, Stress, and relevant Fabrics sections; [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 12 in full; [Introduction to Geology — Perron and Jagoutz, MIT 12.001](../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N), deformation notes and exercise.

Stress describes force per area resolved on oriented surfaces; strain describes changes in geometry under a specified reference and measure. A high strain does not uniquely establish a present high stress. Geological structures record a history involving loading, material properties, fluids, temperature, time, and possibly several overprinting events.

Decompose traction into normal and shear components for an identified plane. Use the structural text's sign and orientation conventions consistently; compression-positive and tension-positive traditions can make identical symbols mean opposite things. State whether a diagram uses total or effective quantities and which fluid-pressure assumption supports it.

Small-strain and finite-strain descriptions differ. Rotations may change an object's orientation without stretching it; accumulated deformation generally cannot be reconstructed simply by adding finite changes as scalars. Distinguish a displacement field, a strain measure, and the pattern of observed markers.

**Work:** complete the stress and kinematics/strain sections and their exercises. Derive normal and shear traction for a simple specified two-dimensional stress state, making the plane orientation and sign convention explicit. Compare two deformation paths that could leave a similar final marker shape and identify additional evidence needed to distinguish them.

Read the complete structural treatment rather than inferring mechanics directly from a photograph of a fold. The broader mechanics trail will deepen tensor methods and constitutive theory; here the geological aim is a defensible connection among observations, geometry, physical model, and deformation history.

**Continue:** [Measure and communicate planes and lines without ambiguous conventions](Orientations-strike-dip-trend-and-plunge.lingua#r_4SACPSFSMY1MM5BV2QM5MJN8QR).

**Return:** [Geology — Earth materials, processes, history, and investigation](../Geology.lingua#r_3CK43VQS79NK0STWNPSCS8YM06).

